68-fd51c57a8d9c"] ["question-28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/id #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465"] ["question-28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/math-academy-id "q-130720"] ["question-28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$"] ["question-28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5"] ["answer-fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212dddc" :answer/id #uuid "fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212dddc"] ["answer-fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212